The `blob` type is `std::basic_string<std::byte>`. `try_decode<blob>` would allocate a `blob`, resize to `decoded_size(str.size())`, then call `decode` with `result.data()` (which is `std::byte*`) on it. But the current `decode` signature takes `void* dst`, so `result.data()` works.

Now let me implement the changes.

### 1. Add `try_decode` to `base64.hpp`

Add after `decode(std::string_view)`:

```cpp
template <class T = std::string>
std::optional<T> try_decode(std::string_view str) {
  auto result = T{};
  result.resize(decoded_size(str.size()));
  auto [written, read] = decode(result.data(), str.data(), str.size());
  if (read < str.size()) {
    if (not std::all_of(str.begin() + read, str.end(),
                        [](unsigned char c) { return c == '='; }))
      return std::nullopt;
  }
  result.resize(written);
  return result;
}
```

Wait, the plan says: "It checks if `read < str.size()`. If so, it verifies that all remaining characters in `str` (from index `read` to the end) are '='. If any are not '=', return `std::nullopt`. If all remaining characters are '=', resize `result` to `written` and return `std::optional<T>{std::move(result)}`. If `read == str.size()`, resize to `written` and return the result directly."

So:

```cpp
template <class T = std::string>
std::optional<T> try_decode(std::string_view str) {
  auto result = T{};
  result.resize(decoded_size(str.size()));
  auto [written, read] = decode(result.data(), str.data(), str.size());
  if (read < str.size()) {
    if (not std::ranges::all_of(str.begin() + read, str.end(),
                                [](char c) { return c == '='; }))
      return std::nullopt;
  }
  result.resize(written);
  return std::optional<T>{std::move(result)};
}
```

But there's a subtlety: `result.data()` for empty T returns a pointer. In the cast.hpp code, the current implementation does `decoded.resize(base64::decoded_size(in.size())); auto [written, read] = base64::decode(decoded.data(), in.data(), in.size());`. `decode`'s first param is `void*`. For `blob`, `result.data()` returns `std::byte*` (or `nullptr` for empty string in C++17, but `std::basic_string::data()` returns non-null pointer since C++11 returns a pointer to the internal buffer even when empty). Actually before C++17 it's undefined, but after C++17 data() returns a usable pointer. Let me not worry; existing cast code does the same thing with blob.

Wait, one issue: `decode(decoded.data(), ...)` where decoded is empty string of bytes — `std::basic_string<std::byte>::data()` returns pointer to internal buffer. Fine.

I need to include `<optional>`. Let me check what's currently included in base64.hpp: `<cstddef>`, `<ranges>`, `<string>`. The plan says "Include `<optional>` if not already included indirectly." I'll add it explicitly.

Also `std::ranges::all_of` requires `<algorithm>` — the `ranges` header shouldn't be relied on for algorithms. Actually `<ranges>` header doesn't guarantee `<algorithm>` availability. To be safe, include `<algorithm>`. Wait, the plan doesn't mention including `<algorithm>`. It suggests using a loop check instead? "it verifies that all remaining characters in str ... are '='". Let me use a simple loop to avoid needing `<algorithm>`:

```cpp
if (read < str.size()) {
  for (size_t i = read; i < str.size(); ++i)
    if (str[i] != '=')
      return std::nullopt;
}
```

That's cleaner and avoids extra includes. Actually the plan says "Include `<optional>` if not already included indirectly." So only optional needs to be added.

Now, one concern about the return type deduction: `return std::optional<T>{std::move(result)};` in both branches. Good.

### 2. Update `cast.hpp`

Replace:

```cpp
  static auto from_str(std::string_view in, const blob_type&)
    -> caf::expected<blob> {
    // TODO: This conversion might be specific to JSON.
    auto decoded = blob{};
    decoded.resize(base64::decoded_size(in.size()));
    auto [written, read] = base64::decode(decoded.data(), in.data(), in.size());
    if (read != in.size()) {
      return caf::make_error(
        ec::convert_error, fmt::format("unable to convert {} into a blob", in));
    }
    decoded.resize(written);
